<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Justinian I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">482–<a href="/years/565">565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Justinian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Justinian I was a Byzantine emperor of the sixth century CE. He ordered the codification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> and reconquered parts of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. His legal work shaped the governance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and preserved the legacy of Roman law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justinian-i.jpg" alt="Justinian I" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_of_Justinianus_I_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_(Ravenna).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to his contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, Justinian was born in the hamlet of Tauresium in the province of Dardania, near the city of Justiniana Prima that he later founded. Later scholarship places his birth around 482. Tauresium is most commonly identified with the archaeological site of Gradište, near the village of Taor southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, though scholars debate the birthplace. From his uncle Justin he inherited ongoing hostilities with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Justinian reigned from 527 to 565 and pursued a restoration of the empire, partly realised. In 532 he concluded an Eternal Peace with Khosrau I at a cost of 11,000 pounds of gold. His generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> then conquered the Vandal Kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the Ostrogothic Kingdom, restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. The emperor himself never took part in campaigns.</p><p>The quaestor Tribonian oversaw the rewriting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>. The first draft of the Codex Justinianeus appeared on 7 April 529, followed by the Digesta in 533, the Institutiones, a textbook, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novellae">Novellae</a>, new laws issued in Greek. In January 532 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> united the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-racing factions against him; he considered flight but stayed, apparently at the urging of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> and Mundus suppressed the revolt, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> says 30,000 unarmed civilians were killed. The destruction allowed him to rebuild the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, completed on 26 December 537. In the early 550s two monks smuggled silkworm eggs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Justinian grew isolated in his last years, and in the 560s he showed clemency to conspirators and pardoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>. He died in his sleep on the night of 14 November 565 and was succeeded by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a>. Procopius, the main source, praised his buildings in one work and portrayed him as cruel and corrupt in the Secret History. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a> is still the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> in many modern states, and many Eastern Christians regard him as a saint.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Justinian I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Avars were a confederation of steppe horsemen who appeared on the edge of the Byzantine world in 558, when their envoys reached <b>Justinian I</b> in Constantinople asking for land and payments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huns, who reached the region in the 370s, appear to have overrun much of its territory and probably ended the monarchy, although the city of Bosporus, the former Panticapaeum, survived and came under Byzantine control in the reign of <b>Justinian I</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Justinian I</b>'s Corpus Juris Civilis consisted of the Codex Justinianus, Digest and Institutes, issued between 529 and 534 CE, and was later supplemented by his Novels (Novellae, new laws).</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Sicily had been reconquered for the Roman Empire by <b>Justinian I</b> in 535 and was a peripheral Byzantine province when Muslim forces from Ifriqiya began raiding it in 652.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Makuria enters written history through Christian missions sent from the Byzantine Empire in the reign of <b>Justinian I</b>. According to the chronicler John of Biclaro, the Makurians accepted Christianity around 569 and sent ivory and a giraffe to the emperor Justin II a few years later, which suggests…</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Justinian I</b>'s Corpus Juris Civilis (529–534 CE), though compiled in the Eastern Empire, preserved and transmitted this tradition to medieval Europe and underlies most modern civil law systems.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=535" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Justinian I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=535">What was here in 535 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The imperial majesty should be armed with laws as well as glorified with arms, that there may be good government in times both of war and of peace…&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes, preface &#183; tr. J. B. Moyle, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5983/pg5983-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Having removed every inconsistency from the sacred constitutions, hitherto inharmonious and confused, we extended our care to the immense volumes of the older jurisprudence; and, like sailors crossing the mid-ocean, by the favour of Heaven have now completed a work of which we once despaired.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes, preface &#183; tr. J. B. Moyle, 1883 &#183; on his codification of Roman law &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5983/pg5983-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Justice is the set and constant purpose which gives to every man his due.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes, book 1 &#183; tr. J. B. Moyle, 1883 &#183; the opening definition, taken from the jurist Ulpian &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5983/pg5983-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The precepts of the law are these: to live honestly, to injure no one, and to give every man his due.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes, book 1 &#183; tr. J. B. Moyle, 1883 &#183; taken from the jurist Ulpian &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5983/pg5983-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…slavery is an institution of the law of nations, against nature subjecting one man to the dominion of another.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes, book 1 &#183; tr. J. B. Moyle, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5983/pg5983-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Freedom, from which men are called free, is a man's natural power of doing what he pleases, so far as he is not prevented by force or law…&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes, book 1, title 3 &#183; tr. J. B. Moyle, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5983/pg5983-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
